window.docContents[40].resolve({"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L3-___-The-body-opens-up--Learning-path":{"contents":"* #* Do* Checked by HCP.L3_TheBodyOpens anchor* Concept disclosed\n* 1* Open body A into left and right halves, index them by pair and body identity, and assemble their masses.* opened_body* HalfOf .P .A names the bearers; mass assembles to the rigid-body whole, while P and Q remain distinct systems.\n* 2* Weight the half positions by this state's half masses, then try to assemble positions by addition.* center_of_mass_mean* centerOfMass_eq uses the OpenBody.mass and .position fields; masses 3 and 5 at positions 0 and 10 give 25/4, not the unit-weight midpoint 5. rigidBodyS has no displacement assembly license.\n* 3* Inspect the massless whole and the nonzero-total condition on a weighted carving.* massless_center_of_mass* PhysLib reports the origin for zero mass; the license is hypothesis h, which a massless state cannot satisfy to build the carving.\n* 4* Check angular velocity over halves and try to assemble it.* angular_velocity_intensive* The halves share ω; adding their angular velocities would double it, so no license exists.\n* 5* Pass lab-frame angular velocity to upstream rotational kinetic energy.* bare_frame_mismatch* The bare expression type-checks even though the inertia tensor is body-fixed, so the accepted term is physically wrong.\n* 6* Apply PKC's frame-indexed contraction to lab and body angular velocities.* framed_rotational_energy* The lab-frame term is refused, the body-frame term is accepted, and the accepted contraction agrees with PhysLib energy.\n* 7* Specialize the old homogeneity assumption to a solid sphere.* solid_sphere* The proved inertia tensor is (2/5) m R² · 1; upstream records the target as informal_lemma CM-RB-sphereI.\n\nWorksheet: HCP/Worksheets/L3_TheBodyOpens.lean · Solution: HCP/L3_TheBodyOpens.lean\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L3 — The body opens up","header":"5.4.6. Learning path","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L3-___-The-body-opens-up--Learning-path"},"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Requirements--Aggregation-_LPAR_R9_RPAR_":{"contents":"R9 — Extensive quantities aggregate additively over parts; intensive ones do\nnot. For an extensive kind the value over a whole is the sum of the values\nover its disjoint parts — the mass of an assembly is the sum of its parts'\nmasses. Where this fails it must not be assumed: volume on mixing is\nsub-additive (ethanol and water), and that negation is stated to keep the\nextensive predicate honest. Tracking which kinds are extensive is the\nprecondition for soundly summing measurements.\n\nBeside the sum and the shared constant sits a third mode that is neither: the\nweighted mean. It is the first aggregation that divides, so it is the first\nthat carries a side condition — the total weight is not zero — and the first\nwhose law does not survive the descent to an executable carrier. The mode is\ncarried at any Carrier, its law is proved where cancellation is available, and\nits rung at genuine binary32 is a bound rather than an equation. Those three\nsentences are one structure read at three carriers, not three structures.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Requirements","header":"2.4. Aggregation (R9)","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Requirements--Aggregation-_LPAR_R9_RPAR_"},"/#annotation-kindCarrier":{"contents":"The audit recognizes a boundary as an application of a carrier structure's constructor or\nfirst-field projection. Quantity and CertifiedQuantity are built in; a downstream layer\nregisters its own — soil-moisture-model registers its DedicatedQuantity, which carries a\nDedicatedKind rather than a KindOfProperty.\n\n/-- A toy single-field carrier, standing in for the model's `DedicatedQuantity`. -/\n@[kindCarrier]\nstructure Tagged (k : KindOfProperty) (R : Type) where\n  magnitude : R\n\n\nRegistering a carrier is the single opt-in that also earns a layer the two structural indexes: the\nkinded-record and kinded-operation tables recognize a structure or definition by whether a\nregistered carrier appears in its fields or its result type. A layer therefore cannot register for\nthe audit and be missed by the indexes, or the reverse.\n\n* Carrier* Origin* What it is* Quantity* built in* A quantity (R10). A magnitude of a fixed kind k, carried at a representation type R.* CertifiedQuantity* built in* A quantity bundled with its discharged admissibility evidence — cert is the gate's receipt, kept in the type rather than asserted at the call site.* NominalValue* built in* §12.4 nominal property value, type-indexed. A designation of the fixed nominal kind k, carried at representation R. The nominal dual of Quantity: same indexing …\n\nWhy the crossing table is not here. The core spine has no kind crossings, and that is a fact\nabout what the spine is rather than an omission: a crossing is where a model meets a measurement,\nso crossings live downstream. The populated table — twenty-three sites, with the kinds each one\ncrosses — is in soil-moisture-model's technical reference. This chapter's boundary examples come\nfrom the validation probe that pins the audit's behaviour.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Using the library: annotations and generated indexes\u0009Where the calculus is left — the boundary family","header":"33.3.6. @[kindCarrier]  — teaching the audit a new carrier","id":"/#annotation-kindCarrier"}});